<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Welland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Welland rises in Northamptonshire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">English Midlands</a> and flows north-east past Market Harborough, Stamford and Spalding to the Wash. For much of its course it forms county boundaries, and its valley was one of the early homelands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-angles">Middle Angles</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-welland.jpg" alt="River Welland" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:River_Welland.18.6.05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Welland is a lowland river some 65 miles (105 km) long. It rises in the Hothorpe Hills at Sibbertoft and reaches The Wash near Fosdyke, where its banks become sea embankments and it ends as a tidal channel, the Welland Outfall. Its catchment covers 609 square miles, of which 179 square miles lie below sea level and would flood without defences. The ground runs from Lias clays in the west through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a> limestone to alluvial soils in the east. Below Spalding it crosses the Fens of South Holland between widely spaced embankments that leave washlands for flood water.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name appears to be older than English, and the river is first mentioned, as the Weolud, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> for 921. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> used it for navigation as far as Stamford, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermine-street">Ermine Street</a> forded it, as part of a system that included the Car Dyke. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, Richard de Rulos, lord of Deeping Fen, built an embankment against floods. A Welland Navigation Act of 1571 complained that mills had made the river impassable, though no work under it is recorded.</p><p>In the 1660s a new cut with 10 locks, the Stamford Canal, was built between Stamford and Market Deeping; it was the longest canal with locks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> at the time. The Draining Deeping Fen Act 1664 granted the Earl of Manchester and others 10,000 acres for drainage works, and from 1730 John Perry built scouring sluices. Drainage succeeded fully only after a steam pumping station was built at Pode Hole in 1827. Traffic on the upper river ended by April 1863, after the railways came, but Spalding remained a port until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Environment Agency is the navigation authority, and the river is navigable up to Crowland. After the floods of 1947 the Coronation Channel was built to protect Spalding, whose silt soils supported large bulb fields from the 1880s. Pinchbeck Marsh Pumping Station, built in 1833, now houses a museum of land drainage. For much of its length the river forms county boundaries, between Northamptonshire and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicestershire">Leicestershire</a> or Rutland and lower down between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridgeshire">Cambridgeshire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Welland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Middle Angles were a group of peoples in the east Midlands, around the Fens and the Nene and <b>Welland</b> valleys.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-middle-angles">Kingdom of the Middle Angles</a></li></ul></section>
